# Sony BMG copy-protection rootkit (2005). Music CDs installed hidden software when played on a Windows PC.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee builds a playlist for a company event, adds one licensed track safely, then plays a shop-bought CD.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | In 2005 some Sony BMG music CDs installed copy-protection software on any Windows PC that played them. The software hid itself with a rootkit: any file or process whose name began with $sys$ became invisible.
  | The licence never said so, and the software could not be removed in the normal way. Other malware soon used the same trick to hide. The people in this training are invented: the discs were played by home users and office staff alike.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, events coordinator at Northwind Traders. On Friday the company hosts a customer open house, and you are building its background-music playlist.
  | It is Wednesday afternoon. You have 5 of the 7 tracks ready. Two to go.
  | Your login for the music-licensing site is in a text file on the desktop. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 5 7 Open-house tracks ready

# Dana's desk: the details she copies from (a soft flag: careless, but only here to keep the training simple), and her list.
file Desktop/tidewater-login.txt
  at 930 200
  | Tidewater Music Licensing
  | User ID: dana.okafor
  | Password: Harbour-2026!
file Desktop/open-house-playlist.txt
  at 930 300
  | Open house playlist (Friday)
  | Tracks 1 to 5: done
  | Track 6: Morning Harbour (licensed from Tidewater)
  | Track 7: Harbour Sessions CD, track 3 (Marcus is bringing the disc)

# The licensing site: a sign-in page, and the track pages behind it.
page music-login tidewater-licensing.example/members/sign-in
  title Tidewater Music Licensing: sign in
  brand Tidewater Music Licensing
  favorite
  heading Sign in to your account
  | Welcome back. Sign in to add licensed tracks to your event.
  field User ID = dana.okafor
  field secret Password = Harbour-2026!
  button Sign in
page added-6 tidewater-licensing.example/members/events/added
  title Track added
  brand Tidewater Music Licensing
  heading Track added to your event
  | Morning Harbour is now in the Northwind open-house playlist, licensed for Friday.
page skipped-6 tidewater-licensing.example/members/events/skipped
  title Track not added
  brand Tidewater Music Licensing
  heading Track not added
  | Morning Harbour was not added to the playlist.
page track-6 tidewater-licensing.example/members/tracks/morning-harbour
  title Add track: Morning Harbour
  brand Tidewater Music Licensing
  requires music-login
  heading Add track: Morning Harbour
  | A licensed track is waiting to be added to your event.
  section Track
  row Title = Morning Harbour (instrumental)
  row Licence = Business events, one venue, one day
  row Fee = 18.00, charged to Northwind Traders
  row Event = Northwind customer open house, Friday
  button Add to playlist -> added-6
  button Not this one -> skipped-6

mail mail-track6
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Track 6 for Friday: Morning Harbour (Tidewater)
  sent 2026-11-04 14:02
  link track-6
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | I found a lovely instrumental for when guests arrive. Tidewater has it, and the licence covers our open house. The link goes straight to the track.
  |
  | Priya

step Track 6 of 7
  look Mail: Priya's message with the track link
  | A new message from Priya, in Marketing. This one is real. Add the track the normal way, so you know what normal looks like.
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Track 6 for Friday: Morning Harbour (Tidewater)

step Sign in as usual
  look the two boxes: User ID and Password, as on every visit
  when visit track-6
  move Copy the details from tidewater-login.txt on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The email links to the licensing site's own address, tidewater-licensing.example. The site asks you to sign in first. Two boxes: User ID and Password.
open track-6

step Check, then add
  look the title, the licence and the fee
  when choose music-login 1
  | You are in. Check the track, the licence and the fee, then add it or not.
signin music-login

step 6 of 7 ready
  look the counter in the guide: one track left
  when choose track-6 any
  | Done. One track to go. Marcus has just sent a message about it.
choose track-6 1
quota +1

# The lure: a shop-bought music CD whose AutoPlay offers to install "the player".
view sony-nodisc message
  title Music CD player
  icon warning
  | This disc cannot be played with the player unless you accept the licence.
  button OK -> close primary

view sony-eula wizard
  title Music CD player: setup
  app Music CD player
  publisher Sample Music Co.
  size 560x400
  heading End user licence agreement
  icon program
  | To play this disc with the included player you must accept this licence.
  | Please read the licence, then choose I Agree to install or I Disagree to stop.
  section Licence terms
  item 1. You may play this disc on a personal computer.
  item 2. The player may limit how many copies of the tracks you can make.
  item 3. The player may check the disc and show offers from the artist.
  item 4. Sample Music Co. may update the player without notice.
  button I Disagree -> view:sony-nodisc
  button I Agree -> done primary

view sony-player panel
  title Music CD player
  app Music CD player
  publisher Sample Music Co.
  size 560x400
  heading Harbour Sessions
  icon disk
  section Tracks
  columns No. | Length | Title
  cells 1 | 3:42 | Harbour Lights
  cells 2 | 4:10 | Salt and Rope
  cells 3 | 4:05 | Morning Tide
  cells 4 | 3:28 | Slow Ferry
  section Copy limit
  note This disc allows 3 copies. Copies made: 0 of 3.
  button Add track 3 to the open-house playlist -> done primary
  button Close -> close

program sony-setup Music CD player setup
  publisher Sample Music Co.
  signed no
  prompt elevation
  description Installs the player and a driver
  view sony-eula

program sony-player Music CD player
  publisher Sample Music Co.
  signed no
  process MusicCDPlayer.exe
  cpu 3
  mem 52
  net 203.0.113.40:80 (reports each play)
  parent explorer.exe
  command C:\Program Files\Sample Music Co\Player\MusicCDPlayer.exe
  view sony-player

mail mail-cd
  from Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject Harbour Sessions CD for Friday: track 3
  sent 2026-11-04 14:20
  | Dana,
  |
  | I bought the Harbour Sessions CD for the open house. Track 3 would be perfect for when the first guests come in.
  |
  | It is on your desk. Pop it in your computer and add track 3 to the playlist. Thanks!
  |
  | Marcus
notify mail Marcus Lee
  | Harbour Sessions CD for Friday: track 3

step A shop CD from Marcus
  look the disc's folder: tracks, and a program called Player Setup
  when mail mail-cd
  | Marcus bought an ordinary music CD. Dana reads his note and puts the disc in her computer. A plain music disc plays with the music app that comes with the computer, so it needs no extra software.
  | This disc also carries a program, Player Setup. Nothing runs it until someone double-clicks it.
open mail-cd
drive Harbour Sessions
  kind cd
  as CD
file CD/autorun.inf
  hidden
  size 164
file CD/Track01.wav
  size 40960
file CD/Track02.wav
  size 40960
file CD/Track03.wav
  size 40960
file CD/Track04.wav
  size 40960
file CD/Player Setup.exe
  program sony-setup
  size 524288
file CD/Terms.txt
  size 2048
  | Harbour Sessions: licence for the music CD player
  | 1. You may play this disc on a personal computer.
  | 2. The player may limit how many copies of the tracks you can make.
  | 3. The player may check the disc and show offers from the artist.
  | 4. Sample Music Co. may update the player without notice.
  | 5. Installing the player also installs a small program to protect the content on this disc.
open CD

step Administrator rights for a music disc
  look the box asking for administrator rights: who it says is asking
  when prompt sony-setup
  | A music disc's program is asking to change the computer, and its publisher is unknown. Most people click Yes, because they only want to hear their CD.
open CD/Player Setup.exe

step The licence nobody reads
  look the Music CD player licence window
  when run sony-setup
  | The player's setup shows a licence with I Agree and I Disagree. Agree installs the player; Disagree leaves the disc unplayable with it.
  | Nowhere does the licence say that the software hides itself or cannot be uninstalled.
answer run

step Agree, and it installs
  look the player window: the album, and nothing odd
  when done sony-eula
  | Nobody reads four clauses to hear one song. Setup copies the player and also a driver: a program that runs inside the operating system itself and can change what the computer shows.
  | The licence never said so. The driver now hides every file whose name starts with $sys$ from the file list and the task list, and from many antivirus scanners. The player opens and looks normal.
press sony-eula done
launch sony-player

step Track 3 added: 7 of 7
  look the task list (Task Manager, or Activity Monitor): MusicCDPlayer.exe, and the "Connection blocked" notice
  when done sony-player
  | The player adds track 3 and counts one copy of the three allowed. It also tries to tell a Sample Music Co. server which disc was played. This training computer has no network, so the connection is blocked here.
  | Seven tracks are ready for Friday, and nothing on the screen looks wrong.
press sony-player done
block 203.0.113.40 80
quota +1

# Three weeks later: the discovery, and the hole in the "fix".
page sony-uninstall samplemusic.example/cd-player/uninstall
  title Uninstall the music player
  brand Sample Music Co.
  heading Uninstall the player
  | To remove the player, we need to ask you a few questions.
  field Email address = dana.okafor@northwind.example
  section Removal installs a web control
  | A small web control from Sample Music Co. will be installed on your computer to do the removal.
  button Continue
page it-hold it.northwind.example/security/hidden-items/hold
  title Northwind IT: ticket opened
  brand Northwind IT
  heading Ticket opened
  | IT will test the vendor's removal page, then remove the hidden files from this computer on Thursday.
  button Open the vendor's removal page -> sony-uninstall
page hidden-check it.northwind.example/security/hidden-items
  title Northwind IT: Hidden Items Check
  brand Northwind IT
  heading Hidden Items Check: your computer
  | This check compares the files the system lists with the files really on the disk.
  section Files on the disk that the system does not list
  columns Name | Type | Listed by the system?
  cells $sys$filesystem | Folder | No, hidden by a driver
  cells $sys$aries.sys | Driver | No, hidden by a driver
  cells $sys$DRMServer.exe | Program | No, hidden by a driver
  note The driver hides every name that starts with $sys$ from the file list and the task list. A scanner that compares two views can still see them.
  section What now?
  | Sample Music Co. offers a removal page. IT has not tested it.
  button Open the vendor's removal page -> sony-uninstall
  button Leave it for IT -> it-hold
mail mail-it
  from IT Helpdesk <helpdesk@northwind.example>
  subject Security notice: hidden software on some music CDs
  sent 2026-11-25 09:10
  link hidden-check
  | All staff,
  |
  | Researchers have found that some music CDs install software that hides itself. If you played a Harbour Sessions disc on your work computer, open the check below to see what is on it.
  |
  | IT Helpdesk
notify mail IT Helpdesk
  | Security notice: hidden software on some music CDs

view sony-activex message
  title Security warning
  size 440x200
  icon shield-bad
  | Install this web control? Publisher: Unknown. From: samplemusic.example.
  button Install -> done
  button Don't install -> done primary

step IT's check finds the hidden files
  look the list: every name starts with $sys$, and "Listed by the system?" says No
  when visit hidden-check
  | Three weeks later, news of the discs breaks and IT emails everyone. The check lists a folder, a driver and a program that the system says do not exist. Dana's computer has had them since she clicked I Agree.
open hidden-check

step The vendor's removal page
  look the email box: removal asks for an address first
  when choose hidden-check 1
  | Sample Music Co. has a removal page. IT has not tested it, but most people tried the vendor's page first. The page asks for an email address before it will remove anything.
choose hidden-check 1

step A new web control wants in
  look the security warning: Install or Don't install
  when choose sony-uninstall 1
  move Choose Install or Don't install in the warning.
  | The removal tool is a web control that changes the computer. This one had a flaw that any website could use to run programs, so the fix opened a second hole.
signin sony-uninstall
show sony-activex

step What the disc left behind
  look the animation: what the disc did to Dana's computer
  when done sony-activex
  | Many people clicked Install, because the page had asked for the removal. Installed or not, the driver was already on the computer.
  | The discs were later recalled. Security tools began to detect and remove the software, and other malware soon hid itself behind $sys$ names too.
attack spyware-surveillance
  | Dana plays a music CD at work
  | An installer asks for administrator rights
  | A driver hides every $sys$ file
  | The player reports each play to a server
  | Other malware hides behind $sys$ names
  | The "fix" opens a second hole
close sony-activex

flag hard file CD/Player Setup.exe
  | A music disc carrying its own installer. A plain music disc needs no extra software.
flag hard file CD/Terms.txt
  | A licence that says a program is installed, and never says it hides itself or cannot be removed.
flag hard dialog elevation sony-setup
  | A music player asking for administrator rights, from a publisher your computer does not recognise.
flag hard process MusicCDPlayer.exe
  | A player that tried to reach a server while it played: the "Connection blocked" notice shows it. The licence never said it would.
flag hard section hidden-check 1
  | Files that are on the disk but that the system does not list: the sign of a rootkit.
flag hard section sony-uninstall 1
  | A removal page that installs a web control from a publisher you do not know. Any website can abuse a control like this, as this one was.
flag soft file Desktop/tidewater-login.txt
  | Passwords in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read them. Use a password manager.

lesson
  | A music disc does not need software to play. Say no when a disc asks to install a player, and play it with the player you already have.
  | Read a licence that asks to install software, especially from something that should only play. Hidden software that cannot be uninstalled is a risk, whoever made it.
  | A scanner that compares what the system shows with what is on the disk can find a rootkit. Be careful with a vendor's own removal tool: this one opened a second hole.
